      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} \\\\\n= \\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ} \\\\\n= \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{3}}{\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 160^\\circ} \\\\\n= \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{3}}{\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cos 20^\\circ} \\\\\n= \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{3}}{\\cos 20^\\circ}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nWe solve the given expression step by step, applying trigonometric identities as needed.\n\nGiven expression:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, we rewrite the expression using a common denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the numerator \\( \\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ \\). This can be done using a trigonometric identity for the angle sum or difference formula. Notice it matches the form for \\(\\sin(\\theta - 60^\\circ)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\theta \\cos \\phi - \\cos \\theta \\sin \\phi = \\sin(\\theta - \\phi).\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\theta = 80^\\circ\\) and \\(\\phi = 60^\\circ\\), so:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 60^\\circ - \\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 60^\\circ = \\sin(80^\\circ - 60^\\circ) = \\sin 20^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nThus, the numerator simplifies to \\(\\sin 20^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 20^\\circ}{\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nWe then relate \\(\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ\\) by another trigonometric identity. Notice that:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 160^\\circ}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin 160^\\circ = \\sin(180^\\circ - 20^\\circ) = \\sin 20^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin 80^\\circ \\cos 80^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 20^\\circ}{2}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 20^\\circ}{\\frac{\\sin 20^\\circ}{2}} = 2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]",
