      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\), we can express it as a single fraction by finding a common denominator. The common denominator for the fractions \\(\\frac{1}{\\cos 80^\\circ}\\) and \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sin 80^\\circ}\\) is \\(\\cos 80^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 80^\\circ\\).\n\nRewriting the expression with the common denominator, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\cdot \\sin 80^\\circ}.\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the numerator \\(\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ\\). To do this, we can use the angle addition formula for sine, but it's easier to use known angle identities. Notice that we can rewrite the numerator as a combination of sine and cosine terms that add up to a known angle. We can use the angle subtraction formula for tangent, which states that for any angle \\(\\theta\\), \\(\\tan(\\theta)\\) can be expressed as the componepcnen of sine and cosine divided by each other. Specifically, \\(\\tan(60^\\circ - \\theta) = \\frac{\\sin(60^\\circ - \\theta)}{\\cos(60^\\circ - \\theta)} = \\frac{\\cos 60^\\circ \\cos \\theta + \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin \\theta}{\\cos 60^\\circ \\cos \\theta - \\sin 60^\\circ \\sin \\theta} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} \\cos \\theta + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin \\theta}{\\frac{1}{2} \\cos \\theta - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\sin \\theta}\\).\n\nIn our case, let \\(\\theta = 80^\\circ\\). Then we have:\n\\[\n\\tan(60^\\circ - 80^\\circ) = \\tan(-20^\\circ) = \\frac{\\sin(-20^\\circ)}{\\cos(-20^\\circ)} = \\frac{-\\sin 20^\\circ}{\\cos 20^\\circ}.\n\\]\nNow we can express \\(\\tan(60^\\circ - 80^\\circ)\\) in terms of sine and cosine to combine with \\(\\cos 80^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 80^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 80^\\circ - \\sqrt{3} \\cos 80^\\circ}{\\cos 80^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ} = \\frac{\\cos(60^\\circ - 80^\\circ)}{1} = \\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}.\n\\]\nTherefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}.\n\\]",
